  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry and warmer weather is expected through the work-\nweek. Confidence in rain and possibly stormy weather continues to \nincrease Saturday into Sunday. \n\nQuick update on potential QPF for the weekend... \n\nCollaboration call with WPC at 200 am revealed the Gulf system will \nlikely be moving more quickly than previous forecasts. QPF maximums \nlikely over the higher terrain in the NC mountains and near the \ninland moving coastal front with this system between 12z Saturday \nand 18z Sunday. Potential faster movement may help keep rainfall a \nbit lower than it could potentially be. Current projections are \naround 1 inch for central NC, with 2-3+ inches near the coastal \nfront and in the mountains. \n\nPrevious discussion...\n\nDry and warmer weather is expected through the end of the week. \nHighs will return to around 80 today and reach the lower to mid 80s \nThursday. This will be the warmest day as SW-W flow will increase \nahead of the approaching dry cold front. The front will push south \nthrough the region Thursday night. There will be little moisture to \nwork with in a deep westerly flow along and ahead of the front. \nThus, no POP. The skies will remain mainly clear/sunny. Cooler high \npressure will build in from the Ohio Valley Friday, then move \noffshore Saturday. There will be increasing cloudiness on Saturday \nwith a chance of showers late, mainly in the south and west. Highs \nwill be in the mid 70s to lower 80s both Friday and Saturday.\n\nThings will quickly change on Saturday into Saturday night as as the \nsystem should be moving quickly. NHC depicts a potential hurricane \napproaching the lower MS valley region by late Friday. This may \ntrack north into the MS/AL areas Saturday. It appears a coastal warm \nfront will exist from NC/SC and potentially move inland Saturday \nnight as the remnant low tracks potentially west of the Appalachians \ninto Sunday. It appears at the current time that the chance of \nshowers will increase by Saturday, becoming likely by Saturday \nafternoon through Saturday night. Showers possibly thunder will \noverspread the region into Sunday. Much more on all the possible \nscenarios in future forecasts.",
